# ── 12 undeclared keywords, found 2026-08-05 ───────────────────
# Same shape as the zen-bin case above, twelve times over. Every package below
# is installed and working, is declared in one of the five sets, and had its
# keyword line in NEITHER this file nor /etc. They were installed by
# `--autounmask-continue` runs whose stray keyword files were later cleaned up
# without these lines being carried across, so nothing recorded why they built.
#
# This was invisible to every check that inspects the RUNNING system.
# `./install.sh --check` was clean, `--depclean --pretend` was empty, all
# twelve were merged and in use. It only appears when portage is asked to
# resolve the sets from scratch, which is precisely what a clean checkout does
# and what this machine never does:
#
#   emerge --pretend --emptytree @world
#
# Note the resolver stops at the FIRST mask, so fixing one reveals the next.
# To get the whole list in one pass, ask about visibility per atom instead:
#
#   for a in $(cat /etc/portage/sets/atlas-* | sed 's/#.*//' | tr -s '[:space:]' '\n' \
#                | grep -E '^[a-z0-9-]+/[a-zA-Z0-9_]' | sort -u); do
#       portageq best_visible / "$a" >/dev/null 2>&1 || echo "NO VISIBLE VERSION  $a"
#   done
#
# Run that after any edit to a set file. It is the only check that answers
# "would this repo actually rebuild this machine?".

